<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Qurra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Qurra was the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-ifran">Banu Ifran</a>, a Zenata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribe of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-revolt">Berber revolts</a> against Arab rule. A Sufri Kharijite, he was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> around 742 and founded the first Ifranid state. He raised large armies and joined attacks on Abbasid governors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, including the siege of Tubna in the 770s.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Qurra belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-ifran">Banu Ifran</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, a Sufri tribe that seized power throughout the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. He led an indigenous Berber Muslim movement with Kharijite tendencies after the overthrow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad dynasty</a>, and is regarded as the first head of state of the Berber Muslim Maghreb. His tribe proclaimed him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, both spiritual and temporal ruler. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> described him in his Kitab al-Ibar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Between 767 and 776 Abu Qurra led expeditions against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> and became their most important enemy in the region; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> credits him with an army of more than 350,000 horsemen. At the head of 40,000 cavalry and infantry, joined by 6,000 Ibadi followers under Ibn Rustam and by troops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> confederation, he besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, where the Abbasid governor had taken refuge. At that time all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes were placed under his command.</p><p>Ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rustam</a>, a Persian married to a woman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-ifran">Banu Ifran</a>, was also proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> by the Banu Ifran and their neighbours the Maghila, and around 778 he took Abu Qurra's place and founded the Rustamid kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiaret">Tahert</a>. Abu Qurra and the Banu Ifran withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, where he allied with the Maghrawa and faced the expansion of the Idrisids. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> sent a strong army under the new governor, Yazid ibn Hatim al-Muhallabi, who defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, though the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> escaped his authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Divisions among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> led Abu Qurra to give up both Kharijism and power. Under the strict rules of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufriyya">Sufrites</a> he would not allow his descendants to found a dynasty, so his kingdom did not last. He welcomed Idris I and recognised his kingdom, breaking with the Rustamids, and Idris I later negotiated the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> with the Maghrawa. In the countryside around Tlemcen the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-ifran">Ifranids</a> kept their heterodox faith, and in 955 their leader Yala Ibn Mohamed revolted against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Qurra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the great Berber revolt against the Umayyad Caliphate from 740, they embraced Sufri Kharijism, and around 742 their leader <b>Abu Qurra</b> established a state at Tlemcen, where he was proclaimed imam.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-ifran-emirate">Banu Ifran emirate</a></li></ul></section>
